---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import LineChart from "../components/LineChart.astro";
import StackedBars from "../components/StackedBars.astro";
import RankedBars from "../components/RankedBars.astro";
import PeriodTable from "../components/PeriodTable.astro";
import { series, latest, valueAt } from "../lib/data.js";
import { num, period } from "../lib/format.js";
import fs from "node:fs";

const ids = ["cpi_headline_index", "cpi_food_bev_index", "cpi_fuel_index", "cpi_core_index",
  "cpi_elec_gas_fuels_index", "cpi_vehicle_fuels_index", "cpi_rural_index", "cpi_urban_index"];
const rows = ids.map((id) => {
  const s = series(id);
  const last = latest(s, "level");
  const prevMonth = s.data.at(-2)?.[0];
  const yearAgo = `${Number(last.period.slice(0, 4)) - 1}${last.period.slice(4)}`;
  return {
    s, last,
    mom: valueAt(s, "mom", last.period),
    yoy: valueAt(s, "yoy", last.period),
    yoyPrev: prevMonth ? valueAt(s, "yoy", prevMonth) : null,
    yoyYearAgo: valueAt(s, "yoy", yearAgo),
  };
});
const latestPeriod = rows[0].last.period;
const published = series("cpi_headline_inflation");
const contrib = JSON.parse(fs.readFileSync("src/data/generated/cpi_contributions.json", "utf8"));
const momentum = JSON.parse(fs.readFileSync("src/data/generated/cpi_momentum_base.json", "utf8"));
const food = JSON.parse(fs.readFileSync("src/data/generated/panels/cpi_food_inflation.json", "utf8"));
const foodLast = food.ref_period, foodCols = food.periods.slice(-6);
const foodBars = food.rows.map((r) => ({ label: r.entity, value: r.values[foodLast] })).filter((r) => r.value !== null && r.value !== undefined);
const foodRows = [...food.rows].sort((a, b) => (b.values[foodLast] ?? -99) - (a.values[foodLast] ?? -99));
const contribTotal = published.data.filter((d) => d[0] >= contrib[0]?.period).map((d) => [d[0], d[1]]);
const wpiYoy = latest(series("wpi_headline_index"), "yoy");
const indent = new Set(["cpi_elec_gas_fuels_index", "cpi_vehicle_fuels_index"]);
---
<Base current="inflation" title="Inflation">
  <h1>Inflation</h1>
  <p class="lede">Is inflation close to RBI's 4% target, and is it broad-based? Consumer prices from MoSPI on the 2024 base, linked to 2013 through MoSPI's official back series. Core CPI follows RBI's definition (excluding food and fuel).</p>

  <LineChart title="CPI inflation, as published" subtitle="% change on a year earlier · RBI target 4% (dashed), tolerance band 2–6% (shaded)"
    lines={[{ id: "cpi_headline_inflation" }]} reference={{ target: 4, band: [2, 6] }} years={12} />
  <p class="chart-sub">Values for 2025 are the rates MoSPI published on the 2012 base; the 2024-base series begins in January 2025 and carries no inflation rates for that year.</p>

  <h2 id="drivers">What is driving inflation</h2>
  <div class="grid2">
    <StackedBars title="Contributions to CPI inflation" subtitle="Percentage points of the 12-month rate; dots show headline inflation as published"
      rows={contrib} total={contribTotal} source="MoSPI; site calculations (Original) with the weights in registry/weights/cpi2024_core.yaml." />
    <LineChart title="Headline, food, fuel and core" subtitle="% change on a year earlier, computed from the indices"
      lines={[{ id: "cpi_headline_index", how: "yoy", label: "Headline" }, { id: "cpi_food_bev_index", how: "yoy", label: "Food and beverages" },
              { id: "cpi_fuel_index", how: "yoy", label: "Fuel" }, { id: "cpi_core_index", how: "yoy", label: "Core" }]}
      reference={{ target: 4 }} years={1} />
  </div>

  <p class="chart-sub"><a href="/inflation/detail/">All twelve divisions of the CPI basket, with their contributions →</a></p>

  <h2 id="food-watch">Food watch</h2>
  <RankedBars title={`Food prices by item, ${period(foodLast, "M")}`} subtitle="CPI inflation, % on a year earlier, as published; dashed line = CPI food and beverages"
    rows={foodBars} reference={latest(series("cpi_food_bev_index"), "yoy")?.value ?? null} referenceLabel="Food & beverages" decimals={1} source="MoSPI, CPI (base 2024), all India, rural + urban." />
  <div class="scroll"><table class="data">
    <thead><tr><th>% y/y</th>{foodCols.map((c) => <th>{period(c, "M")}</th>)}</tr></thead>
    <tbody>{foodRows.map((r) => <tr><td>{r.entity}</td>{foodCols.map((c) => <td>{num(r.values[c], 1)}</td>)}</tr>)}</tbody>
  </table></div>
  <p class="chart-sub">Classes and sub-classes of the CPI food basket, inflation as MoSPI publishes it; on the 2024 base these rates start in January 2026. Vegetable prices swing widely from month to month. Rainfall and reservoir data, planned for this section, are not yet available in a form we can read (IMD publishes them as images).</p>

  <h2 id="momentum">Momentum or base effect?</h2>
  <StackedBars title="Why the inflation rate changed this month" subtitle="Change in 12-month CPI inflation (dots), split into this month's price change and last year's (log points, close to percentage points)"
    rows={momentum.rows} total={momentum.total} source="MoSPI CPI (base 2024, linked back series); site calculation (Original)." />
  <p class="chart-sub">The 12-month inflation rate rises when prices go up this month (momentum) and when the same month last year saw small or falling price changes, which drop out of the comparison (base effect). In logs the split is exact: change in inflation = this month's price change − the price change in the same month a year earlier. Prices are not seasonally adjusted, so momentum can follow a seasonal pattern; the base effect from the same month carries that pattern too. Method: <a href="/methods/">Methods</a>.</p>

  <h2 id="cpi-table">Consumer prices, {period(latestPeriod, "M")}</h2>
  <div class="scroll">
    <table class="data">
      <thead><tr><th>Index (2024=100)</th><th>Index</th><th>1-month % change</th><th>12-month %, latest</th><th>12-month %, previous month</th><th>12-month %, a year ago</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        {rows.map((r) => (
          <tr>
            <td style={indent.has(r.s.id) ? "padding-left:22px" : ""}>{r.s.short_title} {r.s.id === "cpi_core_index" || r.s.id === "cpi_fuel_index" ? <span class="badge">Original</span> : null}</td>
            <td>{num(r.last.value, 2)}</td>
            <td>{num(r.mom, 2)}</td>
            <td>{num(r.yoy, 2)}</td>
            <td>{num(r.yoyPrev, 2)}</td>
            <td>{num(r.yoyYearAgo, 2)}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p class="chart-sub">Changes computed from the published indices; MoSPI's own headline rate for {period(latestPeriod, "M")} is {num(latest(published).value, 2)}%. Not seasonally adjusted. Components below the general index start in January 2025 on the 2024 base, so their 12-month changes start in January 2026.</p>

  <h2 id="core-cpi">How core CPI is built <span class="badge">Original</span></h2>
  <p>RBI defines core inflation as CPI excluding food and fuel, where "fuel" is electricity, gas and other fuels plus petrol, diesel and CNG for vehicles. On the 2024 base, food and beverages weigh 36.75, core 53.04 and fuel 10.21 (of 100). MoSPI does not publish the split of fuel between its two parts in machine-readable form, so we recover it from MoSPI's own indices: each division index is an exact weighted average of its components, and 21 months of data pin the weights down (5.37 and 4.84). Core is then the general index with food and fuel taken out. Checked against RBI's published core index: within 0.01 index points in every month RBI reports (June and July of 2025 and 2026).</p>

  <h2 id="rural-urban">Rural and urban</h2>
  <LineChart title="CPI inflation, rural and urban" subtitle="% change on a year earlier, computed from the indices"
    lines={[{ id: "cpi_rural_index", how: "yoy", label: "Rural" }, { id: "cpi_urban_index", how: "yoy", label: "Urban" }]}
    reference={{ target: 4, band: [2, 6] }} years={12} />

  <h2 id="wpi">Wholesale prices</h2>
  <div class="grid2">
    <LineChart title="WPI and CPI inflation" subtitle="% change on a year earlier, computed from the indices"
      lines={[{ id: "wpi_headline_index", how: "yoy", label: "WPI" }, { id: "cpi_headline_index", how: "yoy", label: "CPI" }]} years={2} />
    <LineChart title="WPI by major group" subtitle="% change on a year earlier"
      lines={[{ id: "wpi_primary_index", how: "yoy", label: "Primary articles" }, { id: "wpi_fuel_power_index", how: "yoy", label: "Fuel and power" },
              { id: "wpi_manufactured_index", how: "yoy", label: "Manufactured" }]} years={2} />
  </div>
  <PeriodTable heading="WPI (2022-23=100), % y/y" n={6} rows={[
    { id: "wpi_headline_index", how: "yoy", label: "All commodities" },
    { id: "wpi_primary_index", how: "yoy", indent: true }, { id: "wpi_food_articles_index", how: "yoy", indent: true },
    { id: "wpi_fuel_power_index", how: "yoy", indent: true }, { id: "wpi_manufactured_index", how: "yoy", indent: true },
  ]} />
  <p class="chart-sub">Office of the Economic Adviser, DPIIT; base 2022-23 from April 2023. WPI inflation for {period(wpiYoy.period, "M")} is {num(wpiYoy.value, 2)}%, which matches OEA's press release for that month (checked on {period("2026-10-28", "D")}; see the series' verification record on the Data page). The latest two months are provisional.</p>

  <h2 id="expectations">Households' inflation expectations</h2>
  <LineChart title="What households expect, and what CPI shows" subtitle="Per cent: median perception and expectations (RBI survey, bi-monthly) and CPI inflation as published"
    lines={[{ id: "iesh_1y_ahead", label: "Expected, one year ahead" }, { id: "iesh_3m_ahead", label: "Expected, three months ahead" },
            { id: "iesh_current_perception", label: "Perceived now" }, { id: "cpi_headline_inflation", label: "CPI inflation" }]} years={2} />
  <p class="chart-sub">RBI's Inflation Expectations Survey of Households covers urban households in 19 cities; results are respondents' views, not RBI's. In every round we hold, perceived inflation was above measured CPI inflation, so the level matters less than the direction.</p>

  <h2 id="cpi-iw">CPI for industrial workers</h2>
  <p class="chart-sub">Labour Bureau's CPI-IW (2016=100): {num(latest(series("cpi_iw_inflation")).value, 2)}% in {period(latest(series("cpi_iw_inflation")).period, "M")}. History is built from each monthly press note (no archive is published), so the series is short. <a href="/data/cpi_iw_index/">Series page</a></p>

  <h2>Still to come on this page</h2>
  <p class="chart-sub">State-wise CPI, momentum vs base effects, and RBI's inflation projections against the outturn. Checklist: docs/guidebook/05-pages/inflation.md.</p>
</Base>
